WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.702
I think, personally, everything that can be easily

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.522
automated is eventually going to be automated

00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:06.682
in the construction industry.

00:00:06.682 --> 00:00:09.062
And automation--I use the term "robot" kind of

00:00:09.062 --> 00:00:11.182
interchangeably for "automation."

00:00:11.182 --> 00:00:14.145
So for the purpose of this talk please,

00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:18.282
give me that and not get too technical about it.

00:00:18.282 --> 00:00:20.442
But it's kind of equivalent to automation in general.

00:00:20.442 --> 00:00:23.902
And also I'm honestly really talking about

00:00:23.902 --> 00:00:25.974
for all intents and purposes weak AI.

00:00:25.974 --> 00:00:29.222
I'm not getting into crazy singularity strong AI,

00:00:29.222 --> 00:00:31.122
what the future could be like a thousand years

00:00:31.122 --> 00:00:37.042
from now. Okay? So this is today.

00:00:37.042 --> 00:00:39.322
Today robots do my drafting.

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:41.342
So I don't draft things anymore.

00:00:41.522 --> 00:00:46.922
There's a whole new thing that came out, started using about 10 years ago.

00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:52.242
This concept called BIM which is Building Information Modeling.

00:00:52.242 --> 00:00:54.262
I'm not going to get too technical here.

00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:56.562
But the basic idea is-- see that top left there?

00:00:56.562 --> 00:00:59.802
That's an image of the 3D model of one of the buildings

00:00:59.802 --> 00:01:02.742
at the UCSF Medical Center, the new medical buildings.

00:01:02.742 --> 00:01:05.292
They are giant machines wrapped in a pretty skin.

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:08.042
So that they're not scary to us people when we go there.

00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:11.162
Like seriously, they're incredibly complex.

00:01:11.162 --> 00:01:13.970
So trying to navigate that kind of a project with

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:14.772
2D drawings on paper

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:16.242
is almost impossible.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:20.022
This whole BIM system basically what you do is

00:01:20.022 --> 00:01:23.042
you describe to the computer in high detail

00:01:23.042 --> 00:01:24.622
what the building is made out of

00:01:24.622 --> 00:01:27.322
and then that system then produces

00:01:27.322 --> 00:01:30.162
a lot of the drawings, plans, sections, elevations...

00:01:30.162 --> 00:01:31.282
all that kind of stuff you need

00:01:31.282 --> 00:01:33.282
to submit to the building department.

00:01:33.282 --> 00:01:35.663
So robots are already doing our drafting.

00:01:35.663 --> 00:01:37.702
Robots are also doing our field measuring.

00:01:37.702 --> 00:01:42.942
This is a laser scan of a city square in Europe.

00:01:42.942 --> 00:01:46.502
With the Wunderkammer cabinet under the stairs here,

00:01:46.502 --> 00:01:49.722
we actually had a point cloud of the interior space

00:01:49.722 --> 00:01:51.122
that somebody had done. And we were able to

00:01:51.122 --> 00:01:53.502
take the laser measurements of that.

00:01:53.502 --> 00:01:56.442
And use that to fabricate the cabinet.

00:01:56.462 --> 00:01:59.142
And the other thing is this whole idea that

00:01:59.142 --> 00:02:03.072
...this is from our friend's company
Tyler, raise your hand?

00:02:03.072 --> 00:02:04.862
(he works for that company)

00:02:04.862 --> 00:02:08.042
This whole idea that it's more about data

00:02:08.042 --> 00:02:10.712
than it is about drawing.

00:02:10.712 --> 00:02:12.162
And also robots build my building.

00:02:12.282 --> 00:02:14.762
So there's this whole concept called digital fabrication

00:02:14.762 --> 00:02:16.482
Where we have these computer-controlled tools

00:02:16.482 --> 00:02:17.462
that cut things out.

00:02:17.462 --> 00:02:19.402
So the things I draw on a computer, that I

00:02:19.402 --> 00:02:22.202
model on a computer: these giant robots will then

00:02:22.211 --> 00:02:23.822
carve or cut out those pieces for me.

00:02:23.822 --> 00:02:25.779
That's how everything you see in here was made.

00:02:25.779 --> 00:02:27.481
We didn't cut out that cabinet.

00:02:27.481 --> 00:02:30.406
That cabinet was cut out for us by a robot.

00:02:30.406 --> 00:02:32.562
Another really interesting thing--

00:02:32.562 --> 00:02:34.282
This is our... one of our CNC machines.

00:02:34.282 --> 00:02:35.382
We have one of the older ones here.

00:02:35.382 --> 00:02:36.382
So this is what I'm talking about.

00:02:36.382 --> 00:02:39.002
It's a giant cutting robot. In the top corner there,

00:02:39.002 --> 00:02:40.675
that's something called a Total Station.

00:02:40.682 --> 00:02:42.382
It's a really exciting new thing

00:02:42.382 --> 00:02:44.721
that's come into existence the last 10 years or so.

00:02:44.722 --> 00:02:47.162
So they started making survey equipment

00:02:47.162 --> 00:02:48.202
for job sites robotic.

00:02:48.202 --> 00:02:51.002
So that one guy could just walk around with a little wand

00:02:51.002 --> 00:02:52.462
and GPS mark points, right?

00:02:52.462 --> 00:02:54.122
And then they're like well if that thing's robotic

00:02:54.122 --> 00:02:55.562
why don't we put appointing laser on it?

00:02:55.562 --> 00:02:57.582
And it can point where stuff's supposed to go.

00:02:57.582 --> 00:02:59.242
So you feed the CAD model to it and it will

00:02:59.242 --> 00:03:00.622
point with a laser, like:

00:03:00.622 --> 00:03:02.341
Oh yeah the anchors for the pipes that you're

00:03:02.342 --> 00:03:04.502
going to hang in 6 months from now go there and

00:03:04.502 --> 00:03:06.622
there and there and there...

00:03:06.622 --> 00:03:08.222
And so it created a feedback loop.

00:03:08.222 --> 00:03:10.242
Because you can measure things on the site, too,

00:03:10.242 --> 00:03:12.242
with that and then feed it back in the CAD model,

00:03:12.242 --> 00:03:14.162
back in the BIM model.

00:03:14.162 --> 00:03:15.402
So if something got put in the wrong place

00:03:15.402 --> 00:03:17.282
you know exactly where it's at, and you can coordinate

00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:18.542
and all that kind of stuff.

00:03:18.542 --> 00:03:19.702
This is all stuff that's happening today.

00:03:19.702 --> 00:03:22.002
This is not hoo-ha future, this is

00:03:22.022 --> 00:03:23.922
really standard kind of stuff.

00:03:24.202 --> 00:03:26.702
Prefabrication and pre-production,

00:03:26.702 --> 00:03:30.602
I'm not talking about the kind of hyped up Dwell like

00:03:30.602 --> 00:03:32.082
we're going to drop a house on a site

00:03:32.082 --> 00:03:35.782
kind of prefabrication. But like bathrooms now

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:37.862
are something on larger commercial projects

00:03:37.862 --> 00:03:39.762
where they're just fabricating those bathrooms

00:03:39.762 --> 00:03:41.142
as little units in a factory and

00:03:41.142 --> 00:03:42.682
just bringing them out and dropping them

00:03:42.682 --> 00:03:44.722
into the site with a crane.

00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:46.532
Also the new Apple campus, the new buildings

00:03:46.532 --> 00:03:48.122
they're building on the Apple campus.

00:03:48.122 --> 00:03:50.022
A bunch of the offices and bathrooms are all

00:03:50.022 --> 00:03:52.522
getting prefabricated in some fancy factory somewhere,

00:03:52.522 --> 00:03:55.122
brought onto the jobsite, dropped in place.

00:03:55.122 --> 00:03:57.692
And a lot of that is heavily automated in those factories.

00:03:57.692 --> 00:04:00.663
And then lastly, this is a bit of a joke,

00:04:00.663 --> 00:04:03.282
but there are robotic bulldozers now.

00:04:03.282 --> 00:04:06.462
Robotic bulldozers look exactly like normal bulldozers

00:04:06.462 --> 00:04:08.922
which is why I put this picture in there instead.

00:04:08.922 --> 00:04:12.682
But basically there are systems now where you

00:04:12.682 --> 00:04:15.562
feed it the CAD data of what the site is supposed to be.

00:04:15.562 --> 00:04:18.122
And a driver just follows a line on a screen

00:04:18.122 --> 00:04:20.142
on a GPS. And the blade of the bulldozer

00:04:20.142 --> 00:04:22.822
automatically moves to grade the site to make it

00:04:22.822 --> 00:04:24.462
have the shape that it needs to have.

00:04:24.462 --> 00:04:26.122
And they've been doing that

00:04:26.122 --> 00:04:27.502
for like 8 years now or something.

00:04:27.502 --> 00:04:30.642
Trimble that has that whole front to back setup.

00:04:30.642 --> 00:04:31.782
It's pretty awesome stuff.

